From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 22:42:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: lg-sw43402: rate-limit brightness to one DCS write
 per frame

This panel is DSI command mode with DSC. A brightness ramp -- an
auto-brightness step when the room lights change, or a finger on the slider --
delivers updates far faster than the 60 Hz frame period, and each one is a DCS
write onto the same link that is carrying the compressed pixel stream. Bursts
of them corrupt the image into horizontal bands of colour that persist until
the panel is re-initialised; a power keypress off and on is the only recovery.

Reproduced on a Pixel 2 XL 2026-08-20: opening the camera and raising the room
light was enough, because that drives auto-brightness. Dragging the slider fast
does it every time; dragging it slowly gives occasional glitches; leaving
brightness alone never does.

This is the policy the vendor stack already used. Downstream MDSS applies the
backlight from the display commit thread, immediately after frame kickoff --
mdss_fb.c: "skip_commit: if (!ret) mdss_fb_update_backlight(mfd);" -- so at
most one DCS write happens per commit, and mfd->unset_bl_level is a single
pending slot, meaning intermediate levels of a ramp collapse and only the
latest is sent. Rate-limiting to one write per frame with the last value
winning reproduces that. The trigger differs: the vendor hangs it off the
commit itself, while a panel driver has no commit hook and uses the frame
period instead.

schedule_delayed_work() is a no-op while the work is already queued, so a burst
collapses into one write per period regardless of how fast userspace writes,
and the work re-reads backlight_get_brightness() when it fires -- brightness is
idempotent, so no pending value has to be stored.

Note the existing dsi-collide instrumentation cannot see this: it samples
MDP_BUSY after the DMA, so it detects write-vs-frame, not write-vs-write. It
reported 0 across the reproduction above, as did the DPU underrun counter.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c | 50 +++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 45 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c
index 13f707ec8a8e..f1e3c7e5db78 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c
@@ -77,6 +77,7 @@ struct lg_sw43402 {
 	struct gpio_desc *reset_gpio;
 	struct regulator_bulk_data supplies[ARRAY_SIZE(lg_sw43402_supplies)];
 	struct delayed_work display_on_work;
+	struct delayed_work bl_work;
 	bool prepared;
 	bool enabled;
 };
@@ -103,12 +104,34 @@ static inline struct lg_sw43402 *to_lg_sw43402(struct drm_panel *panel)
  * Safe while composing only because of msm.cmd_frame_serialize -- see the
  * comment on LG_SW43402_BRIGHTNESS above.
  */
+static void lg_sw43402_bl_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct lg_sw43402 *ctx = container_of(work, struct lg_sw43402,
+					      bl_work.work);
+	u8 brightness;
+	ssize_t ret;
+
+	/* The panel may have been disabled before the delay expired. */
+	if (!ctx->enabled)
+		return;
+
+	/*
+	 * Re-read rather than carrying a pending value: brightness is
+	 * idempotent, so every update that arrived while this work was queued
+	 * collapses into this one write of the latest level.
+	 */
+	brightness = backlight_get_brightness(ctx->panel.backlight);
+
+	ret = mipi_dsi_dcs_write(ctx->dsi, MIPI_DCS_SET_DISPLAY_BRIGHTNESS,
+				 &brightness, sizeof(brightness));
+	if (ret < 0)
+		dev_err(&ctx->dsi->dev, "brightness write failed: %zd\n", ret);
+}
+
 static int lg_sw43402_bl_update_status(struct backlight_device *bl)
 {
 	struct mipi_dsi_device *dsi = bl_get_data(bl);
 	struct lg_sw43402 *ctx = mipi_dsi_get_drvdata(dsi);
-	u8 brightness = backlight_get_brightness(bl);
-	ssize_t ret;
 
 	/*
 	 * Reachable with the DDIC asleep: drm_panel_disable() calls
@@ -118,10 +141,25 @@ static int lg_sw43402_bl_update_status(struct backlight_device *bl)
 	if (!ctx->enabled)
 		return 0;
 
-	ret = mipi_dsi_dcs_write(dsi, MIPI_DCS_SET_DISPLAY_BRIGHTNESS,
-				 &brightness, sizeof(brightness));
+	/*
+	 * Rate-limit to one DCS write per frame.
+	 *
+	 * This panel is command mode with DSC. A brightness ramp -- an
+	 * auto-brightness step, or a finger on the slider -- delivers updates
+	 * far faster than the 60 Hz frame period, and each one is a DCS write
+	 * onto the same link that is carrying the compressed pixel stream.
+	 * Bursts of them corrupt the image into horizontal bands of colour
+	 * until the panel is re-initialised.
+	 *
+	 * schedule_delayed_work() is a no-op when the work is already queued,
+	 * so a burst becomes a single write per period no matter how fast
+	 * userspace writes. The existing dsi-collide instrumentation cannot see
+	 * this case: it samples MDP_BUSY after the DMA, which detects
+	 * write-vs-frame, not write-vs-write.
+	 */
+	schedule_delayed_work(&ctx->bl_work, msecs_to_jiffies(17));
 
-	return ret < 0 ? ret : 0;
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static const struct backlight_ops lg_sw43402_bl_ops = {
@@ -243,6 +281,7 @@ static int lg_panel_disable(struct drm_panel *panel)
 	 */
 	ctx->enabled = false;
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&ctx->display_on_work);
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&ctx->bl_work);
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -446,6 +485,7 @@ static int lg_sw43402_probe(struct mipi_dsi_device *dsi)
 	ctx->dsi = dsi;
 	mipi_dsi_set_drvdata(dsi, ctx);
 	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&ctx->display_on_work, lg_sw43402_display_on_work);
+	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&ctx->bl_work, lg_sw43402_bl_work);
 
 	dsi->lanes = 4;
 	dsi->format = MIPI_DSI_FMT_RGB888;
